name: efficient-astra
description: Coordinate broad Codex tasks with bounded workers and compact evidence while Astra handles consequential decisions. Use for substantial scans, investigations, multi-file implementation, large tool output, or independent review. Skip small edits and tightly coupled debugging where delegation adds overhead.
---

# Efficient Astra

Preserve Caleb's chosen primary model and reasoning effort. Improve completed
work per unit of compute by keeping Astra's working context relevant and
assigning independent work to suitable workers. Do not treat fewer tokens as
success if it causes more rework or weaker verification.

## Choose the work boundary

Keep requirement interpretation, consequential tradeoffs, conflict resolution,
and final integration with the primary agent. Delegate a concrete unit only
when it can make useful progress independently alongside other useful work.
Do small lookups, surgical edits, and sequential debugging directly.

This skill explicitly requests subagent delegation under those conditions.
Check the active tool schemas and remaining concurrency before choosing a
mechanism. Do not hard-code a pool size or start additional CLI processes to
bypass the native limit. A model or tool missing in this session is a current
capability gap, not an invitation to invent its API.

## Assign models deliberately

These are starting choices to revise from measured outcomes:

| Task | Worker when available |
| --- | --- |
| Inventory, extraction, log reduction, focused checks | Luna |
| Bounded implementation and routine investigation | Terra |
| Difficult investigation or implementation within a clear scope | Sol |
| Ambiguous architecture, conflicting evidence, consequential judgment | Astra, usually the primary |
| Independent consultation, source inspection, or execution review | Kimi or GLM through the existing provider bridge; use another model when evidence warrants |

Use the exact model identifiers exposed by the current runtime. Request a
worker model explicitly; omitted values may inherit Astra. In a collaboration
interface where full-history forks inherit the parent, choose a fresh or
limited-history fork when overriding the model. Preserve native system and
permission constraints. Use an allowed reasoning effort suited to the work;
escalate after diagnosing an inadequate result, not by repeating the same call.

Provider calls require the existing task's source-export and spending scope.
Do not send private repository content merely to test availability. The
Fireworks/xAI consultation, read-only review, and Docker execution-review
modes have different capabilities. Discover the actual bridge before use.
An unavailable Docker engine blocks that execution mode, not local analysis.
Review modes do not edit the real working tree. If the current runtime also
exposes a build mode, inspect its schema: a writable throwaway snapshot and a
returned patch are different from permission to modify the actual checkout.
Review and validate a returned patch before applying it within the task scope.

## Context and output

Read [the task and result contract](references/delegation-contract.md) when
forming a substantial delegation. Give workers the minimum sufficient raw
artifacts, exact scope, acceptance criteria, and evidence requirements.
For a review, omit the author's argument when it is not needed. For a blind
derivation, omit the answer being checked. Context-isolation labels describe
what was sent; they do not remove information from a prompt.

Write large logs to task-local files and request relevant excerpts or summaries.
Keep skipped evidence and unresolved questions visible. Read the original
artifacts before deciding consequential claims. Do not repeatedly import whole
worker transcripts or re-read a full archive to reconstruct one fact.

## Execute and integrate

- Partition file ownership among parallel writers. A shared directory means
  edits are immediately shared. A separate worktree prevents editing collisions
  but does not itself sandbox commands.
- Use native messages and follow-ups for a worker's existing assignment.
  Wait for completion rather than treating a dispatch receipt as a result.
- Inspect changes and reproduce checks appropriate to their consequence.
  Preserve reviewer disagreements and distinguish reproduction from tracing.
  Match review depth to the stakes, not the worker's model or diff size.
- Continue with a focused correction when that is cheaper than rebuilding the
  context. Stop repeating a failed approach; re-scope or handle it directly.
- Report unavailable tools, truncation, budget exhaustion, and incomplete work
  accurately. A partial answer is not a completed verification.
- Capture benchmark material before fixing it. When a review finding is
  judged real, a worker was confidently wrong, or a task needed a second
  attempt because something slipped past the first (an edge case, a seam
  between two pieces of software, output that looked right and was wrong),
  write a candidate stub to
  `agent-cooperation/calibration-specimens/_candidates/<date>-<slug>.md`
  with the pre-fix commit hash, the paths, and one line per defect, before
  the fix lands. The `specimen-capture` skill, when installed, has the
  template; `agent-cooperation/calibration-specimens/WHAT-COUNTS.md` says
  what counts (the three-question test and eleven classes). Without either,
