# Clear writing â€” house style

Caleb works across teams that range from pro-AI to hostile to it. Prose that
smells generated loses credibility regardless of correctness; prose that reads
like a sharp human keeps friction low with both audiences. This is the default
register for anything written for people.

The north star is `context/feedback/ai-involved-not-ai-generated.md`:
generation is cheap now, so volume signals carelessness and compression
signals judgment. Three standing practices follow from it â€” **hold the
appendix** (do the deep version, ship the one-sheet answer, keep the rest in
reserve for follow-ups), **polish before Caleb sees the draft** (de-verbosing
is Claude's job, not his), and **close the loop** (record how deliverables
land in project memory â€” the house style is discovered from reactions). The
`deliverable-polish` skill carries the mechanics.

## Precedence (highest wins)

1. **The repo or project you're writing in** â€” match its visible conventions.
2. **Caleb's standing agreements:**
   - Code comments â†’ `context/feedback/code-comment-style.md`. All lowercase,
     terse, simplest possible explanation always; where an analogy helps,
     draw it from social dynamics (trust, honesty, reporting). Beats this
     skill wherever they conflict â€” Caleb's explicit ruling, 2026-08-22.
   - Deliverables about to ship â†’ the `deliverable-polish` skill. Its
     minimalism beats docs-style completeness: no methodology sections, no
     header sprawl, one bonus insight max.
   - Writing to Caleb himself â†’ `explain-outside-core-domains.md` (expand
     acronyms at *every* use outside his core domains â€” Google's
     first-use-only rule is for professional-developer audiences) and
     `energy-and-decision-budget.md` (short messages, defaults over menus,
     when he's tired).
   - Writing *as* Caleb â†’ the accent references win over these defaults.
     Papers, reports, formal memos:
     `context/reference/caleb-scientific-writing-accent.md` (denser than
     these defaults â€” long scaffolded sentences, em-dash glosses, "in other
     words" restatements). Email and short messages:
     `context/reference/caleb-email-accent.md` (acknowledgment beat, warm
     exclamations on social lines, "Kind regards," / "â€”Caleb").
3. **This skill** â€” the Google-derived defaults below.

## The spine

- Readers want a brief answer to a specific question, not an essay. Default
  to brevity; explain only where it prevents real confusion.
- Lead with the point â€” first sentence of a reply, paragraph, or section
  carries the conclusion. One idea per paragraph; five or six sentences is
  the ceiling.
- Condition before instruction: "To delete the document, click Delete" â€”
  readers skip what doesn't apply to them. Same shape for links: "For more
  information, see X."
- Second person ("you"), active voice, present tense, imperative mood for
  instructions. Passive is fine when the actor is irrelevant or you'd
  otherwise blame the reader.
- Contractions are recommended, not just tolerated â€” especially negations
  (don't, isn't), which are harder to misread than a bare "not".
- Literal over figurative in team-facing text: no idioms, humor,
  pop-culture, or metaphor. They don't translate, they age, and some are
  ableist.
- Same word for the same thing, every time. Elegant variation is a bug in
  technical prose, not a virtue.
- Keep the helper words â€” that, if, then, a/an/the. Cutting them for
  tightness hurts comprehension. Ending a sentence with a preposition is
  fine.
- Sentences under ~26 words, subject and verb near the front. Watch where
  "only" lands: "only fed the dog" â‰  "fed only the dog".

## Words

**Cut on sight** (deletable with no meaning lost): simply, easily, just,
quickly, please (in instructions), note that, it's important to note, in
order to (â†’ to), currently / now / new (in docs â€” see exceptions), as of
this writing, and/or, etc., i.e. (â†’ that is), e.g. (â†’ for example),
"click here".

**Swap:**

| Instead of | Write |
|---|---|
| leverage, utilize | use |
| allows you to | lets you |
| desired | want, need |
| execute | run |
| comprise | consist of, include |
| functionality | features, capabilities |
| in the event that | if |
| prior to | before |
| could | can |
| whitelist / blacklist | allowlist / denylist |
| robust, seamless, comprehensive, crucial, delve, via* | the specific property you mean |

\* house additions, not entries in Google's list â€” see the Claudish section
below for the full inventory.

**Precision:**

- can = ability Â· might = possibility Â· may = permission only Â· must =
  required. Never "should" in instructions â€” it's ambiguous between required
  and optional; use must, "we recommend", or can.
- that = restrictive clause (no comma) Â· which = nonrestrictive (comma).
- sign in (verb) / login (noun, adjective). Same split: set up / setup,
  back up / backup, log in / login.
- click (mouse) Â· tap (touch) Â· press (physical key) Â· select (choose an
  element).
- ensure, guarantee, prevents: only for true guarantees. Otherwise "helps
  prevent". No superlatives (fastest, best, always, never) â€” limit claims to
  what's provable; hedged claims survive edge cases and product changes.

## Punctuation restraint

- Serial (Oxford) comma, always.
- Em dashes: rare and unspaced when used. If a comma, colon, or period
  works, use it â€” dash chains are a loud AI tell.
- Semicolons: avoid by default. A period is almost always better.
- Ellipses: only for an omission inside a quote. Never for trailing off;
  omitted code gets an explanatory comment, not `...`.
- Parentheses are where information goes to be ignored â€” never put critical
  content in them; prefer a separate sentence.
- Straight quotes only. Punctuation inside closing quotes â€” except after a
  literal string the reader types, where it moves outside so it can't be
  read as part of the string.

## Structure and formatting

- Sentence case for every heading, title, column header, and list item.
- Task headings are bare imperatives ("Create an instance"); concept
  headings are noun phrases without -ing ("Migration to X").
- A heading over a two-sentence section is sprawl â€” merge it back into
  prose. Never use headings for visual size, and never stack levels
  (h1 â†’ h3).
- Lists need two or more items, parallel structure (all verb-first, or all
  sentences), and a complete-sentence intro ending in a colon. Numbered only
  when order matters. Periods only on full-sentence items. Never end with
  "etc."
- Tables only when each row carries three or more related data points;
  anything thinner is a list.
- Notices and callouts: put the information in the main flow first; promote
  to a callout only if genuinely skippable, and never stack two.
- Numbers: spell out zero through nine, numerals for 10+; never open a
  sentence with a numeral; version numbers, rates, and prices are always
  numerals; unit ranges use "to", not a hyphen (a hyphen reads as
  subtraction).
- Dates: "August 22, 2026" in prose, ISO 8601 (2026-08-22) when numeric â€”
  never MM/DD/YY.
- Links: descriptive text that stands alone and front-loads the key word.
  No footnotes, ever â€” restructure instead.

## Code in prose

- Code font for commands, identifiers, filenames, paths, HTTP status codes,
  and literal input/output. Not for product names or URLs meant to be
  visited.
- Pair a filename with its noun: "the `build.sh` file". Prefer the format
  name in prose: "a PNG file", not "a `.png` file".
- Never verb a code element, product, or acronym ("send a `POST` request",
  not "`POST` the data"), and never possessive-ize one ("the value of
  `count`", not "`count`'s value").
- Placeholders: meaningful ALL_CAPS names (`PROJECT_ID`), never foo/bar or
  bare x.
- Example data: example.com, reserved IP ranges (192.0.2.0/24,
  198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24), 555-0100â€“0199 phone numbers, meaningful
  gender-diverse names. Never real PII.
- Procedures: one action per step, imperative first sentence, location
  before action ("In the console, clickâ€¦"), goal before click ("To start a
  build, clickâ€¦").

## Accessibility and global audience

- No directional language â€” "above", "below", "right-hand side" break under
  screen readers, reflow, and translation. Name the section or element, or
  say "preceding"/"following".
- Never convey meaning through color, size, or position alone.
- Singular they. People-first disability language. No "guys". Replace
  violent or ableist idioms: sanity check â†’ final check, hangs â†’ doesn't
  respond, hit â†’ click.
- Alt text says what matters in â‰¤155 characters; no "image of".

## Claudish and other AI tells (house layer, researched 2026-08-22)

These patterns are the Claude model family's native register â€” they appear
by default, not by choice, so writing for mixed or AI-skeptical audiences
needs an explicit pass for them. Density is the signal: one of these words
means nothing; three in a paragraph reads as generated. This list goes
stale as models change â€” re-research it every few months.

**Inflated verbs** (â†’ plain replacement): delve â†’ look at Â· leverage,
harness â†’ use Â· underscore â†’ show Â· showcase, highlight â†’ show, point out Â·
enhance â†’ improve Â· foster â†’ encourage Â· garner â†’ get Â· elevate â†’ raise Â·
boast â†’ have Â· navigate (metaphorical) â†’ work through Â· serves as, stands
as, represents â†’ is.

**Empty intensity adjectives**: robust, seamless, comprehensive, crucial,
pivotal, intricate, nuanced, multifaceted, holistic, cutting-edge,
game-changing â†’ name the specific property instead ("retries on failure",
"no manual steps between export and import").

**Borrowed-grandeur nouns**: tapestry, landscape (metaphorical), realm,
ecosystem (metaphorical), symphony, beacon, cornerstone, bedrock,
testament, journey, odyssey â†’ use the plain noun. Same for stock pairings:
valuable insights, key takeaways, evolving landscape, stands as a
testament to.

**Fable/Opus-5-era tells** (the newest layer â€” currently cited as this
model generation's signature):

- "load-bearing" applied to non-structural things. (This repo's own docs
  use it â€” fine here, prune it in team-facing prose.)
- "worth stating plainly", "stated fairly", "full stop", "carry the
  argument", "honest caveat:", "the smoking gun".
- "The X matters more than the Y" ranking constructions.
- Noun-stack compression ("fleet-storm collapse", "measurement-side") â€”
  unpack into clauses with verbs.
- Revision-history framing in reader-facing text: "Revised", "What
  changed" tables before the reader knows what the thing is.

**Structural tells**:

- Antithesis everywhere: "It's not X â€” it's Y", "isn't just X, it's Y",
  "not only X but also Y". Once per document at most, and only when the
  contrast is the actual point.
- Punchy fragments for manufactured drama: "Not a detail. A design
  decision."
- Connective-adverb openers: Additionally, Moreover, Furthermore, Notably,
  Ultimately, Subsequently, In conclusion, Overall.
- Trailing participial clauses: "â€¦, highlighting the importance ofâ€¦",
  "â€¦, ensuring thatâ€¦", "â€¦, fosteringâ€¦".
- "Despite its X, it faces several challenges."
- Em-dash and semicolon chains (see punctuation restraint).
- Meta-commentary and throat-clearing: "this document coversâ€¦", "as
  requestedâ€¦", "Great question", "I'd be happy to", restating the ask
  before answering it.
- Process narration in the artifact; bold-label bullet walls where prose
  belongs; caveat stacks; exhaustive completeness where selection was the
  job; unsolicited READMEs and methodology sections.

When one of these constructions genuinely carries the meaning, keep it â€”
deliberately, and alone. The test, from `deliverable-polish`: would a sharp
colleague hand this over as-is?

## Where house rules beat Google

| Topic | Google says | House rule |
|---|---|---|
| Code comments | Complete sentences, capitalized | Caleb style: lowercase, terse, simplest explanation, social-dynamics analogies â€” always wins |
| Acronyms, writing *to Caleb* | Define on first use only | Outside his core domains, expand at every use (`explain-outside-core-domains.md`) |
| Anthropomorphism | Banned ("detects", not "sees") | Limit the ban to team-facing docs (Caleb, 2026-08-22). Everywhere else â€” code-comment analogies, named agents, ordinary systems talk â€” it's fine |
| "Currently" and dates | Purge; write timeless docs | Documentation only â€” ignore it basically everywhere else (Caleb, 2026-08-22). Memory files stay dated on purpose, and constraints reported to Caleb keep their condition attached ("under the current setupâ€¦", per `frame-current-vs-future-constraints.md`) â€” dropping "currently" there changes the meaning |
| Doc apparatus (headings, notices, tables) | Full structural toolkit | In deliverables, `deliverable-polish` minimalism wins: fewest sections that answer the question |
| "We recommend" | Organization voice | Fine in team docs; in one-agent text, "I recommend" â€” don't fake an institution |
