# AI involved, not AI-generated

Caleb, **2026-08-22**:

> "I'm facing a bit of an evolving picture, professionally, where AI adoption
> is on the rise but people are also frustrated by the increasing volume of
> content that this creates. Coworkers are sharing Excel workbooks with seven
> sheets, where before they would have had one. All plots and documents now
> have subtitles, annotations, and README style notes. I think you and I as an
> operating team need to increasingly chase the 'AI involved, but not
> carelessly AI-generated' aesthetic and level of polish."

**The diagnosis:** generation used to be expensive, so volume signaled
effort. Now generation is cheap and reader attention is the bottleneck, so
volume signals that nobody spent judgment on the output â€” the seven-sheet
workbook is unedited AI abundance passed downstream as a reader tax. What
reads as human now is the scarce input: selection, restraint, and local
idiom.

**What the aesthetic is:**

- **The work is smaller than the effort behind it.** One sheet that answers
  the question; the pipeline lives in git for anyone who asks. Depth shows
  in which number was chosen, not how many were shown.
- **A human stands behind every line.** Not concealment â€” Caleb is openly
  honest about AI use. The difference between "AI involved" and "carelessly
  AI-generated" is ownership: everything shipped is something Caleb can
  defend line-by-line. If a line exists only because generating it was
  free, it goes.
- **Local accent over generic polish.** AI output defaults to a generic
  global style; humans carry the conventions of their team. Matching the
  recipient's own formatting habits is both camouflage and respect.

**How to apply:**

- Deliverables: run [[deliverable-polish]]; the verbosity norms in
  [[deliverable-verbosity]] and the bare-noun-phrase title rule in the
  capacity-estimation plot-titles memory are instances of this same
  aesthetic.
- **Hold the appendix instead of shipping it.** Do the extra analysis, keep
  it ready in the session or the repo, send the one-sheet answer. "If they
  ask X, we have it" beats pre-answering everything.
- **De-verbosing is Claude's job, not Caleb's.** He was hand-editing agent
  output down; that stage should disappear. Compress before handing over,
  not after.
- **Capture the feedback loop.** When a deliverable lands well or badly
  with its audience, record one line in that project's memory saying what
  and why. This file is a direction, not a finished spec â€” Caleb expects
  ongoing refinement.
- **Guardrail:** minimalism is a means, not a performance. Annotations that
  exist because someone once got burned can be load-bearing; the test stays
  "does it change what the reader does next", not "is it small". In a data
  share, measured columns are not padding: Caleb ruled on 2026-10-02 that
  resolution beats column count ([[data-share-deliverable]]).

Related: [[deliverable-verbosity]], [[energy-and-decision-budget]],
[[code-comment-style]], the clear-writing skill
(`agent-cooperation/clear-writing/SKILL.md`).
  anything thinner is a list.
- Notices and callouts: put the information in the main flow first; promote
  to a callout only if genuinely skippable, and never stack two.
- Numbers: spell out zero through nine, numerals for 10+; never open a
  sentence with a numeral; version numbers, rates, and prices are always
  numerals; unit ranges use "to", not a hyphen (a hyphen reads as
  subtraction).
- Dates: "August 22, 2026" in prose, ISO 8601 (2026-08-22) when numeric â€”
  never MM/DD/YY.
- Links: descriptive text that stands alone and front-loads the key word.
  No footnotes, ever â€” restructure instead.

## Code in prose

- Code font for commands, identifiers, filenames, paths, HTTP status codes,
  and literal input/output. Not for product names or URLs meant to be
  visited.
- Pair a filename with its noun: "the `build.sh` file". Prefer the format
  name in prose: "a PNG file", not "a `.png` file".
- Never verb a code element, product, or acronym ("send a `POST` request",
  not "`POST` the data"), and never possessive-ize one ("the value of
  `count`", not "`count`'s value").
- Placeholders: meaningful ALL_CAPS names (`PROJECT_ID`), never foo/bar or
  bare x.
- Example data: example.com, reserved IP ranges (192.0.2.0/24,
  198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24), 555-0100â€“0199 phone numbers, meaningful
  gender-diverse names. Never real PII.
- Procedures: one action per step, imperative first sentence, location
  before action ("In the console, clickâ€¦"), goal before click ("To start a
  build, clickâ€¦").

## Accessibility and global audience

- No directional language â€” "above", "below", "right-hand side" break under
  screen readers, reflow, and translation. Name the section or element, or
  say "preceding"/"following".
- Never convey meaning through color, size, or position alone.
- Singular they. People-first disability language. No "guys". Replace
  violent or ableist idioms: sanity check â†’ final check, hangs â†’ doesn't
  respond, hit â†’ click.
- Alt text says what matters in â‰¤155 characters; no "image of".

## Claudish and other AI tells (house layer, researched 2026-08-22)

These patterns are the Claude model family's native register â€” they appear
by default, not by choice, so writing for mixed or AI-skeptical audiences
needs an explicit pass for them. Density is the signal: one of these words
means nothing; three in a paragraph reads as generated. This list goes
stale as models change â€” re-research it every few months.

**Inflated verbs** (â†’ plain replacement): delve â†’ look at Â· leverage,
harness â†’ use Â· underscore â†’ show Â· showcase, highlight â†’ show, point out Â·
enhance â†’ improve Â· foster â†’ encourage Â· garner â†’ get Â· elevate â†’ raise Â·
boast â†’ have Â· navigate (metaphorical) â†’ work through Â· serves as, stands
as, represents â†’ is.

**Empty intensity adjectives**: robust, seamless, comprehensive, crucial,
pivotal, intricate, nuanced, multifaceted, holistic, cutting-edge,
game-changing â†’ name the specific property instead ("retries on failure",
"no manual steps between export and import").

**Borrowed-grandeur nouns**: tapestry, landscape (metaphorical), realm,
ecosystem (metaphorical), symphony, beacon, cornerstone, bedrock,
testament, journey, odyssey â†’ use the plain noun. Same for stock pairings:
valuable insights, key takeaways, evolving landscape, stands as a
testament to.

**Fable/Opus-5-era tells** (the newest layer â€” currently cited as this
model generation's signature):

- "load-bearing" applied to non-structural things. (This repo's own docs
  use it â€” fine here, prune it in team-facing prose.)
- "worth stating plainly", "stated fairly", "full stop", "carry the
  argument", "honest caveat:", "the smoking gun".
- "The X matters more than the Y" ranking constructions.
- Noun-stack compression ("fleet-storm collapse", "measurement-side") â€”
  unpack into clauses with verbs.
- Revision-history framing in reader-facing text: "Revised", "What
  changed" tables before the reader knows what the thing is.

**Structural tells**:

- Antithesis everywhere: "It's not X â€” it's Y", "isn't just X, it's Y",
  "not only X but also Y". Once per document at most, and only when the
  contrast is the actual point.
- Punchy fragments for manufactured drama: "Not a detail. A design
  decision."
- Connective-adverb openers: Additionally, Moreover, Furthermore, Notably,
  Ultimately, Subsequently, In conclusion, Overall.
- Trailing participial clauses: "â€¦, highlighting the importance ofâ€¦",
  "â€¦, ensuring thatâ€¦", "â€¦, fosteringâ€¦".
- "Despite its X, it faces several challenges."
- Em-dash and semicolon chains (see punctuation restraint).
- Meta-commentary and throat-clearing: "this document coversâ€¦", "as
  requestedâ€¦", "Great question", "I'd be happy to", restating the ask
  before answering it.
- Process narration in the artifact; bold-label bullet walls where prose
  belongs; caveat stacks; exhaustive completeness where selection was the
  job; unsolicited READMEs and methodology sections.

When one of these constructions genuinely carries the meaning, keep it â€”
deliberately, and alone. The test, from `deliverable-polish`: would a sharp
colleague hand this over as-is?

## Where house rules beat Google

| Topic | Google says | House rule |
|---|---|---|
| Code comments | Complete sentences, capitalized | Caleb style: lowercase, terse, simplest explanation, social-dynamics analogies â€” always wins |
| Acronyms, writing *to Caleb* | Define on first use only | Outside his core domains, expand at every use (`explain-outside-core-domains.md`) |
| Anthropomorphism | Banned ("detects", not "sees") | Limit the ban to team-facing docs (Caleb, 2026-08-22). Everywhere else â€” code-comment analogies, named agents, ordinary systems talk â€” it's fine |
| "Currently" and dates | Purge; write timeless docs | Documentation only â€” ignore it basically everywhere else (Caleb, 2026-08-22). Memory files stay dated on purpose, and constraints reported to Caleb keep their condition attached ("under the current setupâ€¦", per `frame-current-vs-future-constraints.md`) â€” dropping "currently" there changes the meaning |
| Doc apparatus (headings, notices, tables) | Full structural toolkit | In deliverables, `deliverable-polish` minimalism wins: fewest sections that answer the question |
| "We recommend" | Organization voice | Fine in team docs; in one-agent text, "I recommend" â€” don't fake an institution |
