# Delegation outcome log â€” schema

`log.jsonl` holds **one JSON object per line**. The usual row summarizes one
model on one task. When three reviewers and a builder touch the same task,
that is four rows sharing a `task_id`; `findings_unique` distinguishes them.
Detailed usage can instead use one row per distinct billed attempt, identified
by `usage_source`. Keep measurements disjoint: never log both a task/session
total and its component attempts. Record a finding once, where it was assessed;
do not repeat task-level outcome counts on every attempt. Leave unavailable
outcomes null.

Write rows with `log_task.py` (it validates enums and derives `species`), read
them with `summarize.py`. Nothing else parses this file, so the honest answer
to "I don't know that number" is `null`, never a guess.

## Fields

| field | type | required | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `ts` | ISO 8601 string | auto | when the task ran. Filled with the current local time if omitted; a date-only value (`2026-09-08`) is legal for backfill where the clock time is unknown. |
| `project` | string | yes | repo or memory-project slug, kebab-case (`rrl-db-access`, `watertown-agronomy`). |
| `task_id` | string | yes | one delegated task. Shared by every row of that task â€” the join key for "who else worked this". Convention: `YYYY-MM-DD-short-slug`. |
| `task_shape` | enum | yes | `investigate`, `implement`, `plan-review`, `code-review`, `exec-review`, `blind-derivation`, `consult`, `scan-summarize`, `test-author`, `judge`. See shape notes below. |
| `stakes` | enum | yes | `low` (throwaway, easily reverted), `medium` (internal deliverable), `high` (goes to a customer, a regulator, or production). |
| `role` | enum | yes | `author` (produced the artifact), `reviewer` (checked someone else's work, including a blind re-derivation used as an oracle), `judge` (scored or ranked), `planner` (wrote the spec, plan, or tests). |
| `model` | enum | yes | `fable`, `opus`, `sonnet`, `haiku`, `astra`, `sol`, `terra`, `luna`, `kimi`, `glm`, `grok`, `deepseek`, `gemini` (3.8 Flash), `gemini-pro` (3.1 Pro preview), `gemini-argon` (4 Argon). |
| `species` | derived | auto | `claude` (fable/opus/sonnet/haiku), `gpt` (astra/sol/terra/luna), `kimi`, `glm`, `grok`, `deepseek`, `gemini` (gemini/gemini-pro/gemini-argon). Supply it only if you want it checked; a mismatch is an error. |
| `blindness` | enum | no | `L0` full context (repo, prior discussion, the author's reasoning), `L1` spec plus diff, `L2` blind â€” inputs and the problem statement only, never the answer. |
| `effort` | string | no | free text as passed to the channel: `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `ultra`. |
| `findings_total` | int | no | everything the model reported, before triage. |
| `findings_real` | int | no | findings that named a true problem. |
| `findings_false` | int | no | findings refuted on inspection (wrong facts, misread code, a filter it ignored). |
| `findings_unique` | int | no | real findings **no other model on the same `task_id` produced**. The column that says whether the extra call bought anything. |
| `rework` | enum | no | how much of this model's output had to be redone: `none`, `minor`, `redo`. |
| `duration_s` | number | no | wall clock for the call. |
| `tokens` | int | no | inclusive total tokens. Derived as `input_tokens + output_tokens` only when both are known. A legacy total-only row remains valid. |
| `input_tokens` | int | no | observed inclusive input total. It already includes cache-read and cache-write categories; do not add them again. |
| `output_tokens` | int | no | observed inclusive output total. It already includes reasoning output when the provider exposes that subset. |
| `cached_input_tokens` | int | no | observed cache-read input subset. Missing means unknown, not zero. |
| `cache_write_input_tokens` | int | no | observed cache-write input subset. Missing means unknown, not zero. |
| `reasoning_output_tokens` | int | no | observed reasoning output subset. Missing means unknown, not zero. |
| `model_id` | string | no* | exact provider model/version identifier, preserved verbatim. Required when any detailed usage counter is present. |
| `usage_source` | string | no* | exact provider response, receipt, transcript, or other raw measurement identifier, preserved verbatim. Required when any detailed usage counter is present. |
| `usage_scope` | string | no* | verbatim description of what the counters cover, such as one billed response or the final cumulative session snapshot. Required when any detailed usage counter is present. |
| `cost_usd` | number | no | billed cost if known. |
| `receipt` | string | no | path to the receipt or transcript, relative to the repo that produced it, or the commit hash that is the artifact (the `checkpoint-commits` practice; `cc903e8`, `9d6feb1` and three other rows already do this, and `fast-mode` author rows do). |
| `source` | enum | yes | `live` (logged at task close), `backfill` (reconstructed from memory files â€” counts are as good as the sentence they came from), or `calibration` (scored against a captured specimen). |
| `notes` | string | no | one short line. For backfill, the source sentence fragment, so a reconstructed number can be audited later. |

## Usage invariants

- `cached_input_tokens + cache_write_input_tokens <= input_tokens` when the
  inclusive input total is known.
- `reasoning_output_tokens <= output_tokens` when the inclusive output total is
  known.
- If `tokens`, `input_tokens`, and `output_tokens` are all supplied, `tokens`
  must equal input plus output. Contradictory, negative, boolean, fractional,
  and nonfinite counters are refused.
- A missing counter stays `null`; it is never inferred as zero. Cache and
  reasoning subsets are reported separately and never added to `tokens`.
- For a cumulative session source, record only the final snapshot for that
  scope. Do not sum intermediate cumulative snapshots. Keep separately billed
  retries as separate rows with their original identifiers; the summarizer does
  not deduplicate them.

## Shape notes

The three review shapes differ by what the reviewer could *reach*, because
that is what the measurements keep turning on:

- `consult` â€” packed prompt, no tools. One shot on whatever you pasted.
- `code-review` â€” read-only access to the repo or artifact.
- `exec-review` â€” could run something: a sandbox, the repo's tests, read-only SQL.
- `plan-review` â€” review of a spec or plan *before* the work exists.
- `blind-derivation` â€” L2 re-derivation used as an oracle. Zero findings is the
  *good* outcome here: it means two independent runs agreed. Read this shape's
  rows with that in mind before calling it unproductive.


codex
(1) The five most confident objections

a. The refresh ladder is not yet demonstrated safe for a running session. Empty tools, strict MCP configuration, and a neutral working directory do not disable user hooks. Hooks can execute commands or additional model calls. Claude Code’s own refresh coordination also says nothing about the tray app’s competing writes. Unaddressed, an automatic refresh can trigger unrelated work or race credential replacement. “Cannot race” is unsupported. [Claude Code hook documentation](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks#disable-or-remove-hooks)

b. Antigravity’s failure bounds cover the immediate process incompletely. JsonLineProcess.Dispose skips killing descendants when the parent has already exited. Its parent-only IsAlive test cannot establish tree cleanup. A browser opened through an existing browser process also falls outside that cleanup. The redirected log has no stated size or secret-handling bound. Unaddressed, a timed-out invocation can leave processes, browser activity, or sensitive disk output behind. Microsoft explicitly distinguishes parent exit from descendant termination. [Process.Kill documentation](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.diagnostics.process.kill)

c. Timestamp filtering does not establish which quota window a point belongs to. History lacks account identity, window identity, and the meter’s quota definition. Switching accounts or moving reset boundaries can retain incompatible observations under the same key. The inclusive boundary also admits an old-window observation exactly at the new window’s start. Unaddressed, plausible charts can describe the wrong account or combine different allowance periods.

d. Atomic replacement does not prevent lost updates. Two instances can load the same store, collect different readings, and overwrite each other’s histories. Within one instance, parallel provider completion and shared reader tasks need an explicit serialization and append-once rule. Write failures, unsupported versions, and recovery after an unreadable startup load are unspecified. Unaddressed, restarts can expose missing history or a valid store overwritten from an empty in-memory state.

e. The stale display still makes unsupported claims. “Percent left” sounds current; the tooltip compares an old measurement against today’s elapsed fraction. It can move from above pace to below pace without another observation. Connecting the origin to the first sample invents consumption timing, and the normal flat tail asserts no intervening consumption. Unaddressed, the chart can encourage use based on apparent headroom that was never measured. Label inferred segments and evaluate historical pace at the observation time.

(2) Assumptions to verify before building

- Refresh behavior and authentication selection: test the exact installed version with expired, rejected-but-unexpired, revoked, and missing credentials. Verify which account/configuration the child uses, including environment overrides. An increased expiresAt is not proof that the usage request succeeds.
- Running-session safety: in a controlled account, exercise simultaneous refreshes and timeout cancellation while another Claude session continues. Attribute credential-file writes to processes and verify subsequent authentication. Assess the tray writer separately.
- Hook isolation and cost: install harmless sentinel hooks and inspect child processes and request counts. One requested output word does not establish one request or a few thousand input tokens.
- Antigravity behavior: test expired sign-in, unavailable keyring, unknown slash command, malformed output, and executable upgrades. Confirm these paths cannot silently become model prompts.
- Window semantics: capture consecutive readings through idle activation and an actual reset. Two idle observations do not establish a fixed, zero-based consumption window. One successful auth-status refresh likewise does not justify deleting the fallback for every state.

(3) Missing or untestable acceptance criteria

Define measurable requirements for:

- Zero credential-file writes by the app; explicitly permitted writes by Claude Code; continued usability of the existing session.
- Total ladder duration, cancellation cleanup, manual-click coalescing, and attempt limits across restarts. Once per expiresAt is not a daily spending bound.
- Discovery, pipe overflow, parent-exits-first, surviving descendants, malformed field types, duplicate bucket IDs, and parser exceptions. Every eligible failure must enter cooldown.
- Concurrent store writers, interrupted replacement, disk-full/access-denied saves, unknown versions, account changes, reset-boundary observations, and cached reads that must not become new observations.
- Startup age enforcement and stale tooltips, labels, and accessibility text using the same historical timestamp.

(4) First thing I would probe

Run an Antigravity stand-in that spawns a child holding stdout open, then exits immediately. Let the cap fire. Check the child itself. The supplied Dispose logic skips tree termination once the parent has exited.

(5) Checked and cleared

I found no direct credential-file write in the supplied code or proposed app logic. Shell-free ArgumentList removes the described MSYS conversion path. Stale results preserve the successful read’s timestamp and remove the flat tail. Stable, nonoverlapping windows ordinarily filter correctly, apart from the shared boundary.

This was source and plan review, supported by current documentation. I did not run refreshes, validate the captured Antigravity fixture, or establish live-session safety.
